## Architecture — non-negotiable rules

**Three tiers:**
1. `components/core/` — pure C++ logic, no ESP-IDF or Arduino includes. Compiles for host AND target.
2. `components/adapters_esp32/` — real implementations of the core ports. Only place ESP-IDF headers are allowed.
3. `components/adapters_fake/` — in-memory fakes used by native tests.

**Rules enforced by pre-commit hooks:**
- `components/core/` MUST NOT include `esp_*`, `driver/*`, `freertos/*`, `Arduino.h`, `soc/*`, `hal/*`, `sdkconfig`.
- Every new hardware-touching feature starts with a port (interface) in `components/core/include/ports/`, a real adapter in `components/adapters_esp32/`, a fake in `components/adapters_fake/`, and tests against the fake.
- The composition root (wiring adapters to core) lives in `main/main.cpp` only.

## Test strategy
- Native (fast inner loop): `pio test -e native` — GoogleTest + gMock, runs on WSL2 host.
- Target smoke: `pio test -e test_esp32s3` — Unity on-chip, slower.
[% if hil_instruments %]
- HIL: `pytest -m hil` in `tools/hil/` — drives[% if 'scope' in hil_instruments %] scope[% endif %][% if 'ppk2' in hil_instruments %] + PPK2[% endif %][% if 'sigrok' in hil_instruments %] + sigrok LA[% endif %] against the real board. Opt-in.
[% endif %]
- Python device sim: `uv run pytest tools/sim/tests` — for integration tests with fake peripherals.

## Commands
- Build: `pio run -e esp32s3`
- Flash: `pio run -e esp32s3 -t upload`
- Monitor: `pio device monitor -e esp32s3`
- Native tests: `pio test -e native`
- Target tests: `pio test -e test_esp32s3`
[% if hil_instruments %]
- HIL tests: `uv run pytest -m hil` (from repo root)
[% endif %]
- Python tooling is managed by **uv**. Use `uv run <cmd>` not `python <cmd>`. Add deps with `uv add`, never `pip install`.

## Coding style
- C++, no exceptions, no RTTI (see sdkconfig.defaults).
- Return `core::Result<T, Error>` instead of throwing. The type is `[[nodiscard]]`; never drop a Result on the floor.
- **Result invariant** (exceptions are off, so `std::get` on the wrong variant is UB):
  call `is_ok()` before `value()` and `is_err()` before `error()`. The `code-reviewer` sub-agent rejects unchecked access.
- Interfaces (ports) are `struct` with pure-virtual methods, prefix `I` (IClock, II2cBus).
- Fakes live in `components/adapters_fake/`, prefix `Fake`.
- Real adapters live in `components/adapters_esp32/`, suffix `Esp32` (ClockEsp32, I2cBusEsp32).
- File names: PascalCase for C++ (`IClock.hpp`, `ClockEsp32.cpp`) — one class per file. Python stays snake_case.
- Member variables: trailing underscore (`now_`, `bus_`). Constants: `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE` for codegen output, `kCamelCase` for in-code literals (pick one per file and stay consistent).
- Verbose over terse: `sample_buffer` not `buf`, `i2c_clock_speed_hz` not `spd`.
- clang-format enforced (Google-based, 4-space indent, 100-col). See [`docs/embedded-coding-rules.md`](docs/embedded-coding-rules.md) for the full list of embedded-specific rules (memory, ISR, DMA).

## Hardware knowledge — authoritative sources
- **`hardware/board.yaml`** — pin map, buses, peripheral addresses. Single source of truth for what's on this PCB.
- **`components/board/include/board/Pins.hpp`** — generated from board.yaml. All firmware MUST use these constants. Never raw GPIO numbers. Enforced by pre-commit.
- **`hardware/datasheets/<chip>/`** — per-chip datasheet excerpt (`datasheet.md`), register map, and `<chip>_regs.hpp`. Consult before writing any driver code.
- **`hardware/schematic.png`** — visual reference. Claude reads this with vision when wiring questions arise.
- **`docs/board.md`** — human-authored narrative. Design intent, gotchas, power tree.
- **Per-chip skills** in `.claude/skills/<chip>-driver/` — loaded on demand; list reference libraries; contain known-good driver template.

Onboarding a new chip: user drops datasheet at `hardware/datasheets/<chip>/datasheet.pdf` → run `/component-onboarder` → it produces `datasheet.md`, `register_map.md`, `<chip>_regs.hpp`, and the per-chip skill. Only *then* does `driver-author` write the driver.

Schematic changes: user runs `/board-onboarder` (dry run) → reviews conflicts → `--apply`. See `docs/SCHEMATIC_CONVENTIONS.md` for the required `BOARD_*` attributes and net-naming pattern.

## Agent workflow
- For ad-hoc edits use the sub-agents in `.claude/agents/` explicitly: "use the hal-author sub-agent to add an SPI port."
- Before touching hardware-related code, confirm the port exists in `core/` AND the peripheral exists in `board.yaml`. If either is missing, propose the fix first.
- Before writing a driver, confirm `hardware/datasheets/<chip>/` is populated. If not, stop and run `component-onboarder`.

## Do not
- Do not put `#include <Arduino.h>` or any `esp_*` / `driver/*` header in `components/core/`.
- Do not commit to `main` while native tests fail.
- Do not write directly against `esp_idf` APIs in application logic — go through a port.
- Do not use raw GPIO integer literals — always `board::X_PIN` from `Pins.hpp`. Pre-commit rejects violations.
- Do not hand-edit `components/board/include/board/Pins.hpp` — it regenerates from `board.yaml`.
- Do not transcribe register addresses by hand — use `<chip>_regs.hpp`.
